  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simple conceptual map showing “-omics” as a central hub connected to several major layers of biological analysis. Around the center, cartoon icons represent different data types that together describe biological systems across multiple molecular levels. These include genomics, shown with a DNA double helix; transcriptomics, illustrated by RNA-like strands with bound factors; proteomics, represented by protein chains, complexes, and an antibody; and microbiomics, depicted as bacterial cells.\n\nIt also includes regulatory and specialized omics fields. Epigenomics is shown with DNA wrapped around nucleosomes and “ME” marks, suggesting DNA or chromatin methylation. Cistromics appears as proteins bound to chromatin, implying mapping of DNA-binding factors across the genome. miRNAomics is represented by small RNA hairpin-like structures and a labeled “Dicer,” emphasizing microRNA processing and regulation.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that modern biology integrates many complementary omics approaches rather than relying on one data type alone. The main message is the layered organization of biological information—from genes and chromatin to RNA, proteins, microbes, and small RNAs—and how these domains are interconnected within a broader systems biology framework.",
